IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `qsa-dates`
IMPORT `qsa-rules`

-- Succession Act 1981 (Qld) -- the questions of the CLAIMANT on a deceased person's estate.
--
-- SEAT: the individual with a claim on, or in, the estate of a person who has died -- the surviving spouse or partner,
-- a child or stepchild, a dependant, a relative entitled on intestacy, a beneficiary under the will, a person who may ask
-- to see the will, and the family member or beneficiary concerned with a claim that survives the death (s 66): "Am I the
-- spouse?", "Is this the will?", "Does my gift stand?", "What is my share?", "Can I apply for family provision, and by
-- when?", "Could the court order provision for me?", "Can I still sue over a distribution?", "What can the estate
-- recover?". The court, the personal representative, the registrar, the public trustee and the Crown get no seat: the
-- court's judgments and orders, and what the personal representative has done, are asked as facts.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, numbers, dates, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of qsa-rules.l4.
-- The three time limits (ss 33(2), 39A(3), 41(8)) are a DEONTIC rule run inside the "on this day" questions; no question
-- takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (c) The State of Queensland,
-- CC BY 4.0; not the authorised version. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See
-- NOTES.md.

§ `Succession Act 1981 (Qld): the claimant's questions`

§§ `Where I stand`

@export Am I the spouse of the person who died, under the Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 5AA? Yes if, when they died, you were their husband or wife, their civil partner, or their de facto partner who had lived with them as a couple on a genuine domestic basis for a continuous 2 years ending on the death. For a family provision claim only (Part 4), a former husband, wife or civil partner also counts if they had not remarried or re-partnered before the death and were receiving, or entitled to receive, maintenance from the deceased.
GIVEN `what was I to them when they died` IS A `Relationship with the deceased at the death` @desc Your relationship with the person who died, at the time of the death: husband or wife; civil partner; de facto partner; former husband or wife (divorced); former civil partner (civil partnership terminated); or none of these
      `had we lived together for 2 years` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a de facto partner: whether you had lived together as a couple on a genuine domestic basis for a continuous period of at least 2 years ending on the death
      `is this for a family provision claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you ask for a family provision claim under Part 4 (a former spouse can count only for that)
      `had I remarried or re-partnered before the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a former spouse or civil partner: whether you had married or entered into a civil partnership with someone else before the death
      `was I receiving maintenance from them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a former spouse or civil partner: whether at the death you were receiving, or entitled to receive, maintenance from the deceased
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I the deceased's spouse` MEANS
    `s 5AA -- I am the spouse of the deceased` `what was I to them when they died` `had we lived together for 2 years` `is this for a family provision claim` `had I remarried or re-partnered before the death` `was I receiving maintenance from them`

@export Does a child who was not yet born when the person died count as their child or issue (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 5A)? Yes if the child was conceived before the death, is born alive and remains alive for 30 days; read here as alive on the 31st day after birth. Until then the answer is not yet known.
GIVEN `was the child born alive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child, conceived before the death, was born alive
      `the child's date of birth` IS A DATE @desc The child's date of birth (ISO 8601, e.g. 2026-03-15)
      `has the child died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child has since died
      `the day the child died, or today` IS A DATE @desc The day the child died; if the child is alive, today's date
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does a child born after the death count` MEANS
    `s 5A -- the child unborn at the death counts` `was the child born alive` `the child's date of birth` `has the child died` `the day the child died, or today`

§§ `The will` -- arises only where the person who died left a will, or a document that may be one

@export Is this document the will of the person who died (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) ss 9, 10, 18)? A will is properly made if it is in writing, signed by the will-maker (or by someone else in their presence and at their direction) with the intention of making the will, in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time, at least 2 of whom then sign it in the will-maker's presence. A will made by someone under 18 is not valid, with exceptions. An unsigned or improperly witnessed document that sets out the person's wishes still counts if the Supreme Court is satisfied they intended it to be their will.
GIVEN `was the will-maker under 18 when it was made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died was a minor (under 18) when the document was made
      `does a minor's exception apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a minor's will: whether it was made in contemplation of a marriage that took place, made while the minor was married, made under a Supreme Court order under s 19, or is a foreign will of a minor recognised by s 33X
      `was it made under a court order for a person without capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document is a will made under a Supreme Court order under s 21 (a statutory will for a person without testamentary capacity)
      `is it in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document is in writing
      `was it signed by the will-maker or at their direction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was signed by the will-maker, or by someone else in the will-maker's presence and at their direction
      `was it signed intending to make the will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the signature was made with the intention of executing the will
      `how many witnesses were present together` IS A NUMBER @desc How many witnesses, able to see the will-maker sign, were present at the same time when the will-maker signed or acknowledged the signature
      `how many witnesses signed in the will-maker's presence` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the witnesses attested and signed the will in the will-maker's presence
      `does it set out their wishes for after death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document purports to state the person's testamentary intentions (what is to happen to their property on death)
      `is the court satisfied they meant it as their will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court is (or would be) satisfied that the person intended the document to be their will
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is this document the deceased's will` MEANS
    `ss 9, 10, 18 -- the document is the deceased's will` (`The will document` WITH
        `made while the testator was a minor`                  IS `was the will-maker under 18 when it was made`
        `a s 9(2) or (3) exception covers the minor's will`    IS `does a minor's exception apply`
        `made under a s 21 order`                              IS `was it made under a court order for a person without capacity`
        `in writing`                                           IS `is it in writing`
        `signed by the testator or at the testator's direction` IS `was it signed by the will-maker or at their direction`
        `signed with the intention of executing the will`      IS `was it signed intending to make the will`
        `witnesses present together at the signing`            IS `how many witnesses were present together`
        `witnesses who attested and signed in the testator's presence` IS `how many witnesses signed in the will-maker's presence`
        `states the deceased's testamentary intentions`        IS `does it set out their wishes for after death`
        `the court is satisfied the deceased intended it as the will` IS `is the court satisfied they meant it as their will`)

@export Do I keep a gift in the will although I was a witness to the will, or its interpreter or translator (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) ss 11, 12)? A gift to a witness or interpreter is void, unless at least 2 other witnesses (for a witness) had no gift, everyone who would gain from the gift failing consents in writing, or the Supreme Court is satisfied the will-maker knew and approved of the gift and made it freely. Payment of a debt or proper fees is not a gift.
GIVEN `what was my part` IS A `My part in making the will` @desc Whether you were an attesting witness, or the interpreter or translator used for making the will
      `is it only a debt or fees owed to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will only directs payment of a debt owed to you, or appropriate remuneration for administering the estate or for interpreting
      `how many other witnesses had no gift` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the people who attested the will are not given anything by it
      `did everyone who would gain consent in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether everyone who would benefit if your gift failed has consented in writing, with capacity to consent, to your having it
      `is the court satisfied the will-maker wanted it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court is (or would be) satisfied the will-maker knew and approved of the gift and made it freely and voluntarily
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do I keep my gift although I witnessed or interpreted the will` MEANS
    `ss 11, 12 -- I keep the gift` `what was my part` `is it only a debt or fees owed to me` `how many other witnesses had no gift` `did everyone who would gain consent in writing` `is the court satisfied the will-maker wanted it`

@export Does my gift (or appointment) under a will survive the will-maker's later marriage or civil partnership (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) ss 14, 14A)? A will is revoked when the will-maker marries or enters a civil partnership, except a gift to (or appointment of) the person they were married to or partnered with when they died, a will made in contemplation of that marriage or partnership, and a will stated to be made in contemplation of marriage (or of a civil partnership) generally.
GIVEN `what did the will-maker do` IS A `The testator's later union` @desc Whether the will-maker later married, or entered into a civil partnership
      `was that after the will was made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the marriage or civil partnership came after the will was made
      `is my gift to their spouse or partner at death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your gift or appointment is to the person the will-maker was married to, or in a civil partnership with, when they died
      `was the will made in contemplation of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was made in contemplation of that marriage or civil partnership (whether or not the will says so)
      `what does the will say it contemplates` IS A `What the will says it contemplates` @desc Whether the will states it is made in contemplation of marriage generally, of a civil partnership generally, or neither
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does my gift survive the will-maker's later marriage or civil partnership` MEANS
    `ss 14, 14A -- my gift survives the testator's later union` `what did the will-maker do` `was that after the will was made` `is my gift to their spouse or partner at death` `was the will made in contemplation of it` `what does the will say it contemplates`

@export Does my gift or appointment under my former partner's will survive our divorce, annulment, the end of our civil partnership or the end of our de facto relationship (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) ss 15, 15A, 15B)? These revoke a gift to you in a will that existed when the relationship ended, and your appointment as executor, trustee or guardian and powers of appointment, unless the will shows a contrary intention; but not your appointment as trustee for beneficiaries who include your children, or a power you may use only for your children together. The will then reads as if you had died first.
GIVEN `what did the will give me` IS A `What the will gave me` @desc What the will gave you: a gift of property; an appointment as executor, trustee, advisory trustee or guardian; an appointment as trustee of property on trust for beneficiaries including your children; a power of appointment only in favour of your children together; or another power of appointment
      `did the will exist when we ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will existed when the divorce or annulment happened, the civil partnership was terminated or found void, or the de facto relationship ended
      `does the will show a contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the will (that the gift or appointment is to stand despite the end of the relationship)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does my gift survive the end of my relationship with the will-maker` MEANS
    `ss 15, 15A, 15B -- my gift survives the end of our relationship` `what did the will give me` `did the will exist when we ended` `does the will show a contrary intention`

@export By when must I apply to the Supreme Court to rectify (correct) the will (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 33(2))? Within 6 months after the date of death: the last day is the same date 6 months later (or the last day of that month if it is shorter). The court can extend the time if the estate has not been finally distributed.
GIVEN `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the will-maker died (ISO 8601, e.g. 2026-03-15)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply to rectify the will` MEANS
    `s 33(2) -- the last day to apply to rectify` `the date of death`

@export Can I apply to rectify the will on this day (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 33)? Yes within 6 months after the death; after that only if the Supreme Court extends the time, which it may do if it considers it appropriate and the estate has not been finally distributed. Rectification corrects a will that does not carry out the will-maker's intentions because of a clerical error or because it does not give effect to their instructions.
GIVEN `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the will-maker died (ISO 8601)
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply, or plan to apply, to the Supreme Court (ISO 8601)
      `has the court extended the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has extended (or would extend) the time for your application
      `has the estate been finally distributed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the final distribution of the estate has been made
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to rectify the will on this day` MEANS
    `s 33 -- my application to rectify may be made` `the date of death` `the day I apply` `has the court extended the time` `has the estate been finally distributed`

@export Does a beneficiary or relative who died soon after the person who died still take their gift or share (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) ss 33B, 35(2))? Under a will, a gift to someone who dies within 30 days after the will-maker passes as if they had died first, unless the will shows a contrary intention (a general requirement to survive is not one). On intestacy, a relative must survive the deceased by 30 days. Read here as: they take if they were still alive on the 31st day after the death.
GIVEN `do they take under a will or on intestacy` IS A `How the person takes` @desc Whether the person takes under the will, or on intestacy (no will, or property the will does not deal with)
      `the date of the first death` IS A DATE @desc The date the will-maker or intestate died (ISO 8601)
      `the date the beneficiary died` IS A DATE @desc The date the beneficiary or relative died (ISO 8601)
      `does the will show a contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a will: whether it shows the gift is to take effect even if the beneficiary dies within 30 days (a general requirement that the beneficiary survive the will-maker does not count)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does someone who died soon after the deceased still take` MEANS
    `ss 33B, 35(2) -- the person who died soon after still takes` `do they take under a will or on intestacy` `the date of the first death` `the date the beneficiary died` `does the will show a contrary intention`

@export Am I entitled to inspect, and get a certified copy of, the will of the person who died (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 33Z)? Whoever holds the will (or a copy, if the will is lost, stolen or destroyed) must, if asked, let an entitled person inspect it and give them a certified copy on payment of reasonable expenses. Entitled persons are those mentioned in the will, beneficiaries under an earlier will, the spouse, parents and issue, those who would share on an intestacy, parents or guardians of minors who would benefit, creditors and others with a claim against the estate, and those who may apply for family provision. The will includes a revoked or purported will.
GIVEN `who am I to the will` IS A `Who I am to the will` @desc Which of these you are: a person mentioned in the will; a beneficiary under an earlier will; the will-maker's spouse, parent or issue; a person who would share on an intestacy; the parent or guardian of a minor mentioned in the will or who would share on an intestacy; a creditor or person with a claim against the estate; a person who may apply for family provision; or none of these
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I entitled to see and copy the will` MEANS
    `s 33Z -- I am entitled to inspect and copy the will` `who am I to the will`

§§ `No will: intestacy` -- arises only where the person died without a will, or the will does not deal with all of the property

@export Is this item one of the "household chattels" a surviving spouse takes on intestacy (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 34A)? Household chattels are furniture, furnishings, linen, china, appliances, utensils, garden effects, liquor, consumable stores, domestic animals and other things of ordinary household use or decoration, owned by the deceased immediately before death (including on hire purchase or subject to a security interest); not vehicles, boats, aircraft, racing animals, original works of art, trophies, clothing, jewellery or other personal things.
GIVEN `what is the item` IS A `Kind of item` @desc What kind of item it is
      `did the deceased own it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deceased owned it immediately before death (counting an item on hire purchase or subject to a security interest)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is this item a household chattel` MEANS
    `s 34A -- the item is a household chattel` `what is the item` `did the deceased own it`

@export What is my share, in money, of the estate of a person who died without a will (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 35, ss 36A, 37, schedule 2)? A sole spouse with no issue takes everything; with issue, the spouse takes the household chattels, the first $150,000, and 1/2 of the rest if there is one child (or one line of a child who died) or 1/3 if more, and the issue share the balance. Without a spouse: the issue; then the parents; then brothers and sisters (and children of those who died first); then grandparents; then uncles and aunts (and children of those who died first); then the Crown. A share of someone who died first passes to their issue (for brothers, sisters, uncles and aunts, to their children only). Count only people who survived the deceased by 30 days. The answer leaves out the household chattels and is subject to any family provision order.
GIVEN `what is my place in the family` IS A `My place in the intestate's family` @desc Your place in the family of the person who died: spouse; child; grandchild or other issue whose parent (the deceased's child) died first; parent; brother or sister; child of a brother or sister who died first; grandparent; uncle or aunt; child of an uncle or aunt who died first; or a more remote relative. Half-blood relatives count as whole-blood ones
      `the value of the estate to be shared` IS A NUMBER @desc The residuary estate in Australian dollars: what is left after debts, funeral and administration costs and any gifts in a will, leaving out the household chattels
      `how many spouses survived` IS A NUMBER @desc How many spouses of the deceased survived them (married, civil partner, or de facto partner of at least 2 years)
      `how many children survived` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the deceased's children (including adopted children, and a child born after the death who lived 30 days) survived the deceased by 30 days
      `how many children died leaving issue` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the deceased's children died before them (or within 30 days after) leaving children or other issue who survived the deceased by 30 days
      `how many parents survived` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the deceased's parents survived them by 30 days (0, 1 or 2)
      `how many brothers and sisters survived` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the deceased's brothers and sisters survived them by 30 days
      `how many brothers and sisters died leaving children` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the deceased's brothers and sisters died before them leaving children who survived the deceased by 30 days
      `how many grandparents survived` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the deceased's grandparents survived them by 30 days
      `how many uncles and aunts survived` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the deceased's uncles and aunts (brothers and sisters of a parent) survived them by 30 days
      `how many uncles and aunts died leaving children` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the deceased's uncles and aunts died before them leaving children who survived the deceased by 30 days
      `into how many shares my line divides` IS A NUMBER @desc If you take in place of your parent who died first: how many people share your parent's portion (your parent's children who survived, counting you, and for a grandchild also those who died leaving issue who survived); otherwise 1
      `are the spouses sharing equally` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If more than one spouse survived: whether the spouses' entitlement is to be divided equally (by agreement, court order, or the personal representative under s 36(1)(c))
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is my share of the estate on intestacy` MEANS
    `s 35, schedule 2 -- my share of the residuary estate` `what is my place in the family` (`The intestate's family` WITH
        `surviving spouses`                                     IS `how many spouses survived`
        `children who survived`                                 IS `how many children survived`
        `children who died before, leaving issue who survived`  IS `how many children died leaving issue`
        `parents who survived`                                  IS `how many parents survived`
        `brothers and sisters who survived`                     IS `how many brothers and sisters survived`
        `brothers and sisters who died before, leaving children who survived` IS `how many brothers and sisters died leaving children`
        `grandparents who survived`                             IS `how many grandparents survived`
        `uncles and aunts who survived`                         IS `how many uncles and aunts survived`
        `uncles and aunts who died before, leaving children who survived` IS `how many uncles and aunts died leaving children`) `the value of the estate to be shared` `into how many shares my line divides` `are the spouses sharing equally`

@export Where the person who died left more than one spouse, may the personal representative divide our entitlement equally (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 36(1)(c), (3))? Yes, at the time of distribution, if each spouse was given notice under s 36(3) and 3 months have passed since the last notice, the personal representative has no notice of a written agreement between the spouses, and either has no notice of an application for a court order dividing it, or the spouses have agreed in writing to an equal division, or that application was struck out or discontinued. Any spouse can ask for the notices to be given.
GIVEN `were the notices given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the personal representative gave each spouse the s 36(3) notice
      `the date of the last notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the last of those notices was given (ISO 8601)
      `the date of distribution` IS A DATE @desc The day the personal representative distributes, or proposes to distribute, the entitlement (ISO 8601)
      `do they know of an agreement between us` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the personal representative has notice of a written agreement between the spouses on dividing the entitlement
      `do they know of an application to the court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the personal representative has notice of an application to the court for an order dividing the entitlement
      `did we tell them in writing they may divide equally` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the spouses notified the personal representative in writing that it may divide equally despite that application
      `was the application struck out or discontinued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the personal representative has a copy of a court order striking out or discontinuing the application
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May our spouses' entitlement be divided equally` MEANS
    `s 36(1)(c) -- the personal representative may divide our entitlement equally` `were the notices given` `the date of the last notice` `the date of distribution` `do they know of an agreement between us` `do they know of an application to the court` `did we tell them in writing they may divide equally` `was the application struck out or discontinued`

@export As the surviving spouse, may I elect to buy the home I shared with the person who died (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) ss 39A, 39B)? Yes if their interest in the shared home is not effectively disposed of by a will and you ordinarily lived there when they died; you buy it at its transfer value (market value at death less any mortgage). If the home is part of a larger building or farm in the estate, part of a hotel, motel, boarding house or hostel, or partly used for non-domestic purposes, you may elect only if the Supreme Court makes an order allowing it.
GIVEN `does the will leave the home undisposed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deceased's interest in the shared home is not effectively disposed of by a will (for example there is no will)
      `did I live there as their spouse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were the deceased's spouse and ordinarily lived in the shared home when they died
      `does a s 39B circumstance apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the home forms part of a building, or of agricultural land, the whole of which is in the estate; part of a hotel, motel, boarding house or hostel; or was partly used for non-domestic purposes at the death
      `has the court allowed me to elect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has made an order allowing you to make the election
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I elect to acquire our shared home` MEANS
    `ss 39A, 39B -- I may elect to acquire the shared home` `does the will leave the home undisposed` `did I live there as their spouse` `does a s 39B circumstance apply` `has the court allowed me to elect`

@export By when must I elect to buy our shared home (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) ss 39A(3), 39B(4), (6))? Within 3 months after your appointment if you are a personal representative, or otherwise within 3 months after the personal representative gives you the written notice. If you need a court order first, you must apply for it within the same 3 months, and if it is made your time to elect runs until 1 month after the order (if that is later).
GIVEN `am I a personal representative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a personal representative (executor or administrator) of the estate
      `have I been given the notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are not a personal representative: whether the personal representative has given you the written notice under s 39A(3)(b)
      `the date of my appointment or the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you were appointed personal representative, or the day the notice was given to you (ISO 8601)
      `do I need a court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a s 39B circumstance applies, so that you need the Supreme Court's order allowing the election
      `has the order been made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has made the order allowing you to elect
      `the date of the order` IS A DATE @desc The day the order was made (any date if none)
GIVETH A `Shared home time`
`By when must I elect to acquire our shared home` MEANS
    `ss 39A(3), 39B(4), (6) -- my time to elect` `am I a personal representative` `have I been given the notice` `the date of my appointment or the notice` `do I need a court order` `has the order been made` `the date of the order`

@export Is my election to buy our shared home in time if I give it on this day (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) ss 39A(3), 39B)? It must be made within 3 months after your appointment as personal representative or after the personal representative's notice to you, extended to 1 month after any court order allowing it; where you need a court order, you cannot elect until it is made.
GIVEN `am I a personal representative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a personal representative (executor or administrator) of the estate
      `have I been given the notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are not a personal representative: whether the personal representative has given you the written notice under s 39A(3)(b)
      `the date of my appointment or the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you were appointed personal representative, or the day the notice was given to you (ISO 8601)
      `do I need a court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a s 39B circumstance applies, so that you need the Supreme Court's order allowing the election
      `has the order been made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has made the order allowing you to elect
      `the date of the order` IS A DATE @desc The day the order was made (any date if none)
      `the date I elect` IS A DATE @desc The day you give, or plan to give, the written election (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my election to acquire the shared home in time` MEANS
    `s 39A(3) -- an election on` `the date I elect` `is in time under` (`ss 39A(3), 39B(4), (6) -- my time to elect` `am I a personal representative` `have I been given the notice` `the date of my appointment or the notice` `do I need a court order` `has the order been made` `the date of the order`)

§§ `Family provision` -- arises where the will or the intestacy does not adequately provide for a spouse, child or dependant

@export Am I a "dependant" of the person who died, for a family provision claim (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 40)? Yes if they were wholly or substantially maintaining or supporting you when they died (not for full payment in return) and you are their parent, the parent of their surviving child under 18, or under 18 yourself.
GIVEN `were they supporting me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when they died, the deceased was wholly or substantially maintaining or supporting you, otherwise than for full valuable consideration
      `am I their parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a parent of the deceased
      `am I the parent of their child under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the parent of a child of the deceased who survived them and was under 18
      `was I under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were under 18 when the deceased died
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a dependant of the deceased` MEANS
    `s 40 -- I am a dependant of the deceased` `were they supporting me` `am I their parent` `am I the parent of their child under 18` `was I under 18`

@export Am I a stepchild of the person who died, for a family provision claim (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 40A)? Yes if you are the child of their spouse, unless the step-relationship stopped because they and your parent divorced, ended their civil partnership or ended their de facto relationship. It does not stop because your parent died first while still with them, or because they re-partnered after your parent's death.
GIVEN `my parent and the deceased` IS A `My parent and the deceased` @desc Your parent's relationship with the deceased: your parent was the deceased's spouse when the deceased died; the marriage, civil partnership or de facto relationship lasted until your parent died before the deceased; it ended in divorce, termination or separation; or your parent was never the deceased's spouse
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a stepchild of the deceased` MEANS
    `s 40A -- I am a stepchild of the deceased` `my parent and the deceased`

@export Can I apply to the Supreme Court for family provision from the estate of the person who died (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 41(1), ss 5AA, 40, 40A)? You can if you are their spouse (including a de facto partner of 2 years, and a former spouse or civil partner who was receiving or entitled to maintenance), their child, stepchild or adopted child, or their dependant. Whether the court will order anything is a separate question.
GIVEN `who was I to them` IS A `Who I was to the deceased` @desc Who you were to the person who died: husband or wife; civil partner; de facto partner; former husband or wife (divorced); former civil partner; child or adopted child; child of their spouse or partner; parent; or someone else
      `had we lived together for 2 years` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a de facto partner: whether you had lived together as a couple on a genuine domestic basis for a continuous period of at least 2 years ending on the death
      `had I remarried or re-partnered before the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a former spouse or civil partner: whether you had married or entered into a civil partnership with someone else before the death
      `was I receiving maintenance from them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a former spouse or civil partner: whether at the death you were receiving, or entitled to receive, maintenance from the deceased
      `my parent and the deceased` IS A `My parent and the deceased` @desc For a child of their spouse or partner: your parent's relationship with the deceased (as in the stepchild question)
      `were they supporting me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when they died, the deceased was wholly or substantially maintaining or supporting you, otherwise than for full valuable consideration
      `am I the parent of their child under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the parent of a child of the deceased who survived them and was under 18
      `was I under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were under 18 when the deceased died
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply for family provision` MEANS
    `s 41(1) -- I may apply for family provision` (`Family provision applicant` WITH
        `who I was`                                            IS `who was I to them`
        `lived together for 2 years ending on the death`       IS `had we lived together for 2 years`
        `remarried or partnered before the death`              IS `had I remarried or re-partnered before the death`
        `receiving or entitled to maintenance at the death`    IS `was I receiving maintenance from them`
        `my parent and the deceased`                           IS `my parent and the deceased`
        `maintained by the deceased at the death`              IS `were they supporting me`
        `a parent of a surviving child of the deceased under 18` IS `am I the parent of their child under 18`
        `under 18 at the death`                                IS `was I under 18`)

@export By when must I start my family provision application (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 41(8))? Unless the Supreme Court directs otherwise, the application is heard only if the proceedings are started within 9 months after the death (an application filed by anyone else counts as yours on this time limit, s 41(6)): the last day is the same date 9 months later (or the last day of that month if it is shorter). To stop the personal representative distributing safely after 6 months, give them signed written notice of your intended application (s 44).
GIVEN `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person died (ISO 8601, e.g. 2026-03-15)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I start my family provision application` MEANS
    `s 41(8) -- the last day to start a family provision application` `the date of death`

@export Can my family provision application be heard if I start it on this day (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 41(8))? Yes if the proceedings are started within 9 months after the death; after that only if the Supreme Court directs that it be heard. If someone else filed an application in time, it is deemed to be made on behalf of everyone who might apply, so far as the time limit goes (s 41(6)); answer as of the day that application was filed.
GIVEN `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person died (ISO 8601)
      `the day I start` IS A DATE @desc The day you start, or plan to start, the proceedings in the Supreme Court (ISO 8601)
      `does the court direct it be heard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a late application: whether the Supreme Court directs (or would direct) that it be heard anyway
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my family provision application be heard` MEANS
    `s 41(8) -- my application may be heard` `the date of death` `the day I start` `does the court direct it be heard`

@export Could the Supreme Court order provision for me out of the estate of the person who died (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 41)? It may, on your application as their spouse, child or dependant, if the will or intestacy does not make adequate provision for your proper maintenance and support; for a dependant only if it is satisfied provision is proper; not if it refuses you for your character, conduct or circumstances; not for a late application unless it directs otherwise; and, if you need a declaration of paternity, not until you have it. The court's views are your inputs; the answer is whether it may order provision, not how much.
GIVEN `who was I to them` IS A `Who I was to the deceased` @desc Who you were to the person who died: husband or wife; civil partner; de facto partner; former husband or wife (divorced); former civil partner; child or adopted child; child of their spouse or partner; parent; or someone else
      `had we lived together for 2 years` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a de facto partner: whether you had lived together as a couple on a genuine domestic basis for a continuous period of at least 2 years ending on the death
      `had I remarried or re-partnered before the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a former spouse or civil partner: whether you had married or entered into a civil partnership with someone else before the death
      `was I receiving maintenance from them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a former spouse or civil partner: whether at the death you were receiving, or entitled to receive, maintenance from the deceased
      `my parent and the deceased` IS A `My parent and the deceased` @desc For a child of their spouse or partner: your parent's relationship with the deceased (as in the stepchild question)
      `were they supporting me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when they died, the deceased was wholly or substantially maintaining or supporting you, otherwise than for full valuable consideration
      `am I the parent of their child under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the parent of a child of the deceased who survived them and was under 18
      `was I under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were under 18 when the deceased died
      `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person died (ISO 8601)
      `the day I start` IS A DATE @desc The day you started, or plan to start, the proceedings (ISO 8601)
      `does the court direct it be heard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a late application: whether the Supreme Court directs that it be heard anyway
      `was adequate provision not made for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that the will or intestacy does not make adequate provision for your proper maintenance and support
      `is the court satisfied provision is proper` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a dependant: whether the court is satisfied, given how far the deceased supported you, your need for that support to continue and the circumstances, that some provision is proper
      `does the court refuse me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court refuses you because your character or conduct disentitles you, or your circumstances make refusal reasonable
      `do I need a declaration of paternity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim depends on a declaration of paternity under the Status of Children Act 1978
      `have I obtained it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have obtained that declaration
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Could the court order provision for me` MEANS
    `s 41 -- the court may order provision for me`
        (`Family provision applicant` WITH
            `who I was`                                            IS `who was I to them`
            `lived together for 2 years ending on the death`       IS `had we lived together for 2 years`
            `remarried or partnered before the death`              IS `had I remarried or re-partnered before the death`
            `receiving or entitled to maintenance at the death`    IS `was I receiving maintenance from them`
            `my parent and the deceased`                           IS `my parent and the deceased`
            `maintained by the deceased at the death`              IS `were they supporting me`
            `a parent of a surviving child of the deceased under 18` IS `am I the parent of their child under 18`
            `under 18 at the death`                                IS `was I under 18`)
        (`Family provision hearing` WITH
            `the day of death`                                      IS `the date of death`
            `the day the application was started`                   IS `the day I start`
            `the court directs that a late application be heard`    IS `does the court direct it be heard`
            `adequate provision was not made for my proper maintenance and support` IS `was adequate provision not made for me`
            `the court is satisfied provision for me as a dependant is proper` IS `is the court satisfied provision is proper`
            `the court refuses because of my character, conduct or circumstances` IS `does the court refuse me`
            `I need a declaration of paternity`                     IS `do I need a declaration of paternity`
            `I have obtained the declaration of paternity`          IS `have I obtained it`)

@export What property can a family provision order reach (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) ss 5B, 41(12))? The estate, plus money or property the deceased gave away in contemplation of death (a donatio mortis causa), plus property over which the deceased had a general power of appointment by will. Give each in Australian dollars.
GIVEN `the value of the estate` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the deceased's estate, in Australian dollars
      `gifts made in contemplation of death` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of money or property the deceased gave as a gift in contemplation of death (donatio mortis causa), in Australian dollars (0 if none)
      `property under a general power of appointment` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of property over which the deceased exercised, or could exercise, a general power of appointment by will, in Australian dollars (0 if none)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What property can a family provision order reach` MEANS
    `ss 5B, 41(12) -- the estate for family provision` `the value of the estate` `gifts made in contemplation of death` `property under a general power of appointment`

@export Can I still sue the personal representative over a distribution of the estate they have made, given that I may claim family provision (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 44)? No action lies over a distribution properly made: for the maintenance or support of the spouse or a dependent child; after you told them in writing you consent or will not apply; on or after 6 months from the death if they had no signed written notice of any family provision application or intended application on the estate (yours or anyone else's); or, if they had such a notice, on or after 9 months from the death unless they had been told in writing that the application was started, or were served with it. The answer is "No" where the section protects them, and not answered otherwise.
GIVEN `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person died (ISO 8601)
      `the date of distribution` IS A DATE @desc The day the personal representative distributed the property (ISO 8601)
      `was it properly made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the distribution was otherwise properly made by the personal representative
      `was it for the spouse's or a dependent child's maintenance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was made to provide for the maintenance or support of the deceased's spouse or a child of the deceased who was wholly or partly dependent on them immediately before the death
      `did I tell them in writing I consent or will not apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (being of full legal capacity) told the personal representative in writing before the distribution that you consent to it or do not intend to apply in a way affecting it
      `had they signed written notice of any claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before the distribution, the personal representative had written notice, signed by the applicant or their lawyer, of any family provision application or intended application on the estate (yours or another person's)
      `had they been told it was started or served with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before the distribution, the personal representative had written notice that the application had been started in the court, or had been served with a copy of it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I still sue the personal representative over a distribution` MEANS
    `s 44 -- I can sue the personal representative over the distribution` (`the distribution facts` `the date of death` `the date of distribution` `was it properly made` `was it for the spouse's or a dependent child's maintenance` `did I tell them in writing I consent or will not apply` `had they signed written notice of any claim` `had they been told it was started or served with it`)

@export Can a family provision order undo a distribution the personal representative has already made (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 44(1))? No, if it was properly made to provide for the maintenance or support of the deceased's spouse or a child dependent on them, whether or not the personal representative knew of a claim. The Act does not say otherwise for other distributions.
GIVEN `was it properly made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the distribution was properly made by the personal representative
      `was it for the spouse's or a dependent child's maintenance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was made to provide for the maintenance or support of the deceased's spouse or a child of the deceased who was wholly or partly dependent on them immediately before the death
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can a family provision order disturb the distribution` MEANS
    `s 44(1) -- the distribution can be disturbed by a family provision order` (`the distribution facts` (`the calendar date` 1 1 2000) (`the calendar date` 1 1 2000) `was it properly made` `was it for the spouse's or a dependent child's maintenance` FALSE FALSE FALSE)
    -- The dates and the notices are fixed placeholders: s 44(1) reads none of them.

§§ `Deaths together` -- arises only where two people died and it is uncertain which died first

@export When two people died and it is uncertain which of them died first, who is taken to have survived the other (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 65)? Unless the court orders otherwise, the deaths are presumed to have happened in order of age: the younger is taken to have survived the elder by 1 day. Under a will, a beneficiary who survived by only 1 day is treated as having died first (s 33B: 30 days).
GIVEN `is it uncertain who died first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deaths happened in circumstances making it uncertain which person survived the other
      `has the court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has made an order about the order of the deaths
      `the first person's date of birth` IS A DATE @desc The first person's date of birth (ISO 8601)
      `the second person's date of birth` IS A DATE @desc The second person's date of birth (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Who is taken to have survived`
`Who is taken to have survived` MEANS
    `s 65 -- who is taken to have survived` `is it uncertain who died first` `has the court ordered otherwise` `the first person's date of birth` `the second person's date of birth`

§§ `Claims that survive a death` -- arises where the person who died had a claim, or a claim was brought against their estate

@export Can the estate of the person who died recover this kind of damages in a claim they had when they died (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 66)? Claims survive for the estate, except for defamation or seduction; but the estate cannot recover damages for pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm or loss of expectation of life (except in a dust-disease claim the deceased had started before dying from or partly from the disease, not caused by tobacco), or exemplary damages; and where the wrong caused the death, nothing for losses or gains to the estate from the death (funeral expenses apart) or for the deceased's future earnings. Family members' own claims for a death are under the Civil Proceedings Act 2011 Part 10.
GIVEN `what kind of damages` IS A `Kind of damages` @desc The kind of damages asked about
      `is the claim for defamation or seduction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deceased's cause of action was for defamation or seduction
      `did the wrong cause the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by the act or omission the claim is about
      `is it for a dust-related condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is for personal injury (including disease) resulting from a dust-related condition (as defined in the Civil Liability Act 2003, schedule 2, e.g. asbestosis, mesothelioma, silicosis)
      `does it result from tobacco` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products, or exposure to tobacco smoke
      `had the deceased started proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deceased started a court proceeding on the claim before dying
      `did the condition cause or contribute to the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deceased died as a result of the dust-related condition, or it was a contributing factor
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the estate recover these damages` MEANS
    `s 66 -- the estate can recover` `what kind of damages` (`The surviving action` WITH
        `it is for defamation or seduction`                    IS `is the claim for defamation or seduction`
        `the death was caused by the act or omission sued on`  IS `did the wrong cause the death`
        `it is for personal injury from a dust-related condition` IS `is it for a dust-related condition`
        `the injury results from tobacco`                      IS `does it result from tobacco`
        `the deceased started a proceeding before dying`       IS `had the deceased started proceedings`
        `the condition caused or contributed to the death`     IS `did the condition cause or contribute to the death`)

@export I received part of an estate, and someone with a claim against the person who died is suing me: what is the most a judgment against me can be (Succession Act 1981 (Qld) s 66(6), (9))? A claim that survives against the estate may be brought against a beneficiary to whom part of the estate was distributed, but a judgment against the beneficiary can never exceed the amount distributed to them. You may claim contribution or indemnity from other beneficiaries, and the court may relieve you if you received it in good faith and changed your position.
GIVEN `did I receive part of the estate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether part of the estate was distributed to you
      `how much was distributed to me` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount (value) distributed to you, in Australian dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most a judgment against me as a beneficiary can be` MEANS
    `s 66(6), (9) -- the most a judgment against me can be` `did I receive part of the estate` `how much was distributed to me`
